PRIOR ART

The divisibility criterion implemented by this software is not original to this
project. It is Pascal's divisibility criterion, described by Blaise Pascal in
"De numeris multiplicibus ex sola characterum numericorum additione
agnoscendis" (written c. 1654, published 1665). The trimming test this project
calls "the 1997 equation" belongs to the family published by A. Zbikowski in
"Note sur la divisibilite des nombres" (Bulletin de l'Academie imperiale des
sciences de St. Petersbourg, 3:151-153, 1861), and is one member of the
two-parameter family of A. A. Grinberg and S. Luryi, "General Divisibility
Criteria" (arXiv:1401.5486, 2014). The equivalence of the trimming and summing
families of tests is due to E. O'Shea, "Divisibility Tests Unified: Stacking
the Trimmings for Sums" (Mathematics Magazine; arXiv:1903.04903, 2019).

Lemi Orhan Ergin arrived at the 1997 equation independently, as a high school
student in Izmir in 1997, and at the generalized rule independently in 2026,
in both cases without knowledge of the works above. This is an independent
rediscovery, not a discovery.

What this project contributes is: a closed form for the trimming coefficients
read off the divisor's last digit alone; the exactness criterion
gcd((y*a2)+1, d) = 1 for that parametrization and the list of divisors below
100 where it fails; and this division-free, fully traced, exhaustively tested
implementation together with its four-language interactive presentation.

A claim-by-claim mapping to the literature is in the "Prior Art and Related
Work" section of README.md.
See https://github.com/lemiorhan/grand-unified-divisibility-rule

